1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,420
Every morning, my alarm goes off, I wake up from my slumber and hit the snooze button.
每天早上，我的闹钟响起，我从沉睡中醒来，按下贪睡延时按钮。

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:09,980
Then I crawl back under my duvet and catch a few more minutes of shut-eye.
然后我又爬回被子里面，再多睡上几分钟觉。

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,730
"I'll go to bed early tonight,"
“今晚我要早点睡觉。”

4
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:12,820
I tell myself.
我对自己说。

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:18,250
But then, in the evening, I get a second wind and completely forget about my early night.
但是，到了晚上，我又重振雄风，完全忘记了要早睡这件事。

6
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:22,950
Why am I finding it so difficult to get a good night's sleep?
为什么我觉得睡个好觉这么难呢？

7
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:24,930
And is it a problem?
这算是个问题吗？

8
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:30,290
The amount we sleep has declined over the years and insomnia is on the rise.
这些年来我们的睡眠时间减少了，失眠的情况却在增加。

9
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:33,020
Modern technology is often blamed.
现代科技常常被认为是罪魁祸首。

10
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:42,210
The light from our smartphone affects levels of melatonin - the sleep-inducing hormone - keeping us wide awake into the early hours.
我们智能手机发出的光会影响褪黑素的水平——褪黑素是一种催眠的激素——让我们一直到凌晨都还十分清醒。

11
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:46,040
Lack of sleep can seriously damage our health and memory.
睡眠不足会严重影响我们的健康和记忆力。

12
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:51,690
We need deep sleep to transfer our memories from our short-term storage to our long-term storage.
我们需要深度睡眠来将我们的记忆从短期存储转移到长期存储中。

13
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:55,200
If we don't sleep enough, we may lose those memories.
如果我们睡眠不足，就可能会失去这些记忆。

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,380
This is particularly disastrous for people studying for exams.
这对正在备考的人来说尤其糟糕。

15
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:02,600
So, how can we learn to sleep better?
那么，我们怎样才能学会更好地睡眠呢？

16
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:09,100
For Professor Till Roenneberg, it's important people recognise they have an internal body clock.
对于蒂尔·罗内伯格教授来说，重要的是人们要认识到自己有一个内在的生物钟。

17
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:14,180
This determines whether you are a night owl, an early bird or somewhere in the middle.
这决定了你是一个夜猫子、早起的人，还是介于两者之间。

18
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,260
We don't have any choice.
我们别无选择。

19
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:24,180
"It's like feet," he said "Some people are born with big feet and some with small feet, but most people are somewhere in the middle."
“这就像脚一样，”他说，“有些人天生脚大，有些人天生脚小，但大多数人都介于两者之间。”

20
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:28,770
Our work schedules are out of sync with our natural sleep patterns.
我们的工作时间表与我们自然的睡眠模式不同步。

21
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:35,750
He says this leads to "social jetlag" where people feel like they are constantly in the wrong time zone.
他说这会导致“社交时差”，让人感觉自己总是处于错误的时区。

22
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:42,000
Paul Kelley - a sleep expert from the University of Oxford - says that most people are getting up too early.
牛津大学的睡眠专家保罗·凯利说，大多数人起床太早了。

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,110
He believes work and school should start a few hours later.
他认为工作和上学都应该晚几个小时开始。

24
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:53,400
Many companies are starting to realise more sleep can mean a more productive workforce and are changing work schedules.
许多公司开始意识到，更多的睡眠意味着更有生产力的劳动力，并且正在改变工作时间表。

25
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:02,090
While in South Korea, office workers are heading to relaxation parlours at lunchtime to take power naps in hammocks or massage chairs.
而在韩国，上班族会在午餐时间去休闲场所，在吊床或按摩椅上小睡。

26
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:08,360
This is much healthier than catching forty winks while sitting on the toilet or dropping off at their desks.
这比坐在马桶上打个盹或者在办公桌上睡着要健康得多。

27
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:14,560
So, if like me you find yourself hitting your snooze button every morning - don't feel bad.
所以，如果你和我一样，发现自己每天早上都在按贪睡按钮，别难过。

28
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:16,180
It's just our body clocks!
这只是我们的生物钟在作祟！
